[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Dependency Injection (DI) and Inversion of Control (IoC) in C#"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dependency Injection (DI) and Inversion of Control (IoC) are design patterns that promote loose coupling between software components. They are fundamental principles in modern C# development, leading to more maintainable, testable, and flexible code."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Inversion of Control (IoC)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"IoC is a design principle where the control of object creation and dependencies is transferred to a container or framework. Instead of a class creating its dependencies, the dependencies are provided to the class from an external source. This 'inverts' the traditional control flow."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Dependency Injection (DI): The Implementation of IoC"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"DI is a specific way of implementing IoC. It focuses on how dependencies are provided to a class. There are three main types of DI:"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Constructor Injection: Dependencies are provided through the class's constructor."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Property Injection: Dependencies are provided through public properties of the class."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Method Injection: Dependencies are provided through methods of the class."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Constructor Injection Example"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"This is the most common and recommended type of DI. It ensures that the class receives all its required dependencies at the time of creation."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// Interface defining the dependency
public interface ILogger
{
    void Log(string message);
}

// Concrete implementation of the dependency
public class ConsoleLogger : ILogger
{
    public void Log(string message)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(message);
    }
}

// Class that depends on ILogger
public class MyService
{
    private readonly ILogger _logger;

    // Constructor injection
    public MyService(ILogger logger)
    {
        _logger = logger ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(logger));
    }

    public void DoSomething()
    {
        _logger.Log("MyService is doing something...");
    }
}

// Usage (typically handled by an IoC container)
ILogger logger = new ConsoleLogger();
MyService service = new MyService(logger);
service.DoSomething();"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Property Injection Example"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dependencies are injected via public properties. This is less common than constructor injection but can be useful in certain scenarios."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"public class MyService
{
    public ILogger Logger { get; set; }

    public void DoSomething()
    {
        // Ensure Logger is not null before using it
        Logger?.Log("MyService is doing something...");
    }
}

// Usage (typically handled by an IoC container)
MyService service = new MyService();
service.Logger = new ConsoleLogger();
service.DoSomething();"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Method Injection Example"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dependencies are injected via a method. This is the least common type of DI."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"public class MyService
{
    private ILogger _logger;

    public void SetLogger(ILogger logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public void DoSomething()
    {
        _logger?.Log("MyService is doing something...");
    }
}

// Usage (typically handled by an IoC container)
MyService service = new MyService();
service.SetLogger(new ConsoleLogger());
service.DoSomething();"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"IoC Containers in C#"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"IoC containers (also known as DI containers) are frameworks that automate the process of creating and managing dependencies. They handle the instantiation of objects and the injection of dependencies, reducing boilerplate code and improving maintainability. Popular C# IoC containers include:"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection (built-in to .NET Core and .NET 5+)"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Autofac"},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ninject"},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"StructureMap (now deprecated, consider alternatives)"},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection"},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;

// Configure the service collection
var serviceCollection = new ServiceCollection();
serviceCollection.AddTransient<ILogger, ConsoleLogger>(); // Register ILogger with ConsoleLogger
serviceCollection.AddTransient<MyService>(); // Register MyService

// Build the service provider
var serviceProvider = serviceCollection.BuildServiceProvider();

// Resolve the dependency
var myService = serviceProvider.GetService<MyService>();

// Use the service
myService.DoSomething();"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `AddTransient` registers the services. `AddTransient` creates a new instance of the service every time it's requested. Other options include `AddScoped` (creates a new instance within the scope) and `AddSingleton` (creates a single instance for the lifetime of the application)."},{"order":28,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of DI and IoC"},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Increased Testability: Dependencies can be easily mocked or stubbed for unit testing."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reduced Coupling: Components are less dependent on concrete implementations, promoting modularity."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improved Maintainability: Changes to one component are less likely to affect other components."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Increased Reusability: Components can be easily reused in different contexts."},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dependency Injection and Inversion of Control are powerful design patterns that can significantly improve the quality of your C# code. By embracing these principles and utilizing IoC containers, you can create more robust, maintainable, and testable applications."}]
